      "pageEnd": 11,
      "target": "../sections/page-011.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "WHO/GPA/TCO/HCS/95.15\n\nWhen he nervously arrived at the hospital, the doctor there greeted him warmly and\nteferred him to have a conversation with one of the nurses in her office. In the course of the\nconversation, the nurse asked him why he wanted an HIV test. She listened carefully as he\nexplained his recent sexual history and described his fears. She then asked him what actions he\nmight take if the result of the HIV test turned out to be positive. They also discussed what it\nwould imply to be HIV-positive and what he would do in the interim to protect his wife in case\nhe was already HIV-inf",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Tells the story of a man who sought an HIV test and was counselled by a nurse about his sexual history, fears and the test, illustrating pre-test counselling. It explains the window period when people are infectious but test antibody negative.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
