      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 38",
      "pageStart": 38,
      "pageEnd": 38,
      "target": "../sections/page-038.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Counselling for HIV/AIDS: A key to caring\n\nATV counselling and testing\n\nIn order to offer national AIDS programmes guidance on the role of HIV testing, a\nconsultation was held in Geneva in November 1992 (see the statement listed in Annex 1). Its\npurpose was to review what is known about the advantages and disadvantages of HIV testing,\nand to develop recommendations on the role of testing and counselling in HIV/AIDS\nprevention and care programmes. The consultation stressed that testing programmes which\ndo not require and secure an individual’s informed consent could damage efforts to prevent\nHI",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Summarises a 1992 WHO consultation on HIV testing, which warned that testing without informed consent harms prevention while voluntary testing with counselling can support both seropositive and seronegative people. It then considers how future developments, such as new testing products and better clinical care prolonging life, could raise demand for HIV/AIDS counselling.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-58534/blocks/prose-page-039",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 39",
      "pageStart": 39,
      "pageEnd": 39,
      "target": "../sections/page-039.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "WEHO/GPA/TCO/HCS/95.15\n\nLarge-scale trials of vaccines against HIV will lead to increased demand for\nHIV/AIDS counselling services. All research protocols for vaccine trials\nshould ensure provision of counselling services for subjects who are involved\nin the study; such provision should include funds for the necessary training in\nHIV/AIDS counselling. In countries where vaccine trials are to be conducted,\nplanners will need to put into place strategies to ensure that the increased\ndemand for care-providers with counselling skills is met.\n\n31",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Notes that large HIV vaccine trials will increase demand for counselling and that trial protocols and national planners should provide for counselling services and training.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-58534/blocks/prose-page-040",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 40",
      "pageStart": 40,
      "pageEnd": 40,
      "target": "../sections/page-040.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Counselling for HIV/AIDS: A key to caring\n\nIn summary\n\nHIV/AIDS counselling services:\n\nare necessary to enable affected and worried individuals and families to cope with the\n\neffects of HIV infection and with the stress of receiving HIV testing;\n\n- should relate and be linked to other HIV/AIDS activities;\n\n- are usually delivered by providers who have received in-service training in\ncounselling skills to supplement their basic professional training;\n\n- should be established at all sites where care is provided for people with HIV infection\nand where linked HIV testing is carried out;\n\n- should",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Summarises Part Two of the guide, stating that HIV/AIDS counselling helps people cope with infection and testing, should be linked to other HIV activities and integrated into existing services, and is usually delivered by staff with in-service counselling training.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-58534/blocks/prose-page-041",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 41",
      "pageStart": 41,
      "pageEnd": 41,
      "target": "../sections/page-041.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "WHO/GPA/TCO/HCS/95.15\n\nPART THREE: THE ROLE OF POLICY-MAKERS AND\nPLANNERS\n\nWhat are the essential policies and procedures that should be in place before HIV/AIDS\ncounselling services are established?\n\nThe HIV/AIDS counselling process is most effective when the following are in place\nprior to the establishment of services:\n\n- policy statements regarding the objectives and purpose of HIV/AIDS\ncounselling services in the country or institution, including where and to\nwhom counselling should be delivered, and the strategies that are to be\nfollowed to ensure that counselling is available/accessible",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Opens Part Three on the role of policy-makers and planners, listing policies needed before counselling services start, such as clear objectives, confidential or anonymous testing procedures, voluntary testing with informed consent and rules on who provides counselling. It stresses that policy-makers can play a key role and that a review of existing policies may be needed.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-58534/blocks/prose-page-042",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 42",
      "pageStart": 42,
      "pageEnd": 42,
      "target": "../sections/page-042.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Counselling for HIV/AIDS: A key to caring\n\nlisted below represents one approach to solving the problem of non-existent or inappropriate\n\npolicies:\n\n1. Nomination of a coordinator for HIV/AIDS counselling and collection of relevant\nbackground information.\n\n2. Identification and prioritization of issues in HIV/AIDS counselling that require policy\ndevelopment or amendment.\n\n3. Detailed analysis of priority issues and selection of appropriate policies to address the\nissues,\n\n4, Development of draft policy statements.\n\n5. Presentation to and review of draft policies by senior officials.\n\n6. Finaliz",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Sets out seven steps for developing HIV/AIDS counselling policy, from naming a coordinator and identifying issues through drafting, review, dissemination and monitoring. It describes the first step, including who should take part in the policy process, such as AIDS committee members, planners, service providers, clinicians and NGOs.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-58534/blocks/prose-page-043",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 43",
      "pageStart": 43,
      "pageEnd": 43,
      "target": "../sections/page-043.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "WHO/GPA/TCO/HCS/9S. 15\n\nStep Two:\n\nThe coordinator will also be responsible for preparing or commissioning the\npreparation of a report on the current status of counselling services in the\ncountry, region or institution for which policies are to be developed. This\nreport will include:\n\n- up-to-date epidemiological information on HIV/AIDS and a brief\ndiscussion of the implications of this information for the policy-makers\nand planners of HIV/AIDS counselling services;\n\n- an assessment of the existing, and likely future, demand for HIV/AIDS\ncounselling services;\n\n- a description of existing servi",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the coordinator's situation report on epidemiology, demand and existing counselling services and training, with supporting documents. It explains how priority policy issues may differ between high-prevalence settings, where staffing matters most, and low-prevalence settings, where confidentiality and partner notification may dominate.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-58534/blocks/prose-page-044",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 44",
      "pageStart": 44,
      "pageEnd": 44,
      "target": "../sections/page-044.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Counselling for HIV/AIDS: A key to caring\n\nStep Three:\n\nStep Four:\n\nThe list of issues identified is then circulated to all participants in the policy\ndevelopment process and each participant asked to rank the items on the list in\norder of importance, The totals are then collated and analysed and the results\nused for selecting priority policy issues. It is suggested that only the first five\nor six items on the list should be included in the final list of priority issues.\n\nDetailed analysis of priority issues and selection of appropriate policy\ntools.\n\nEach priority issue should then be examine",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains how participants rank policy issues to select five or six priorities and how each is analysed, with examples of general and specific policies and the means to implement them, such as legislation. It notes that written policies are then drafted, though implicit policies may be used where public opposition delays formal ones.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
