      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 50",
      "pageStart": 50,
      "pageEnd": 50,
      "target": "../sections/page-050.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Counselling for HIV/AIDS: A key to caring\n\nTesting with informed consent: HIV testing performed only after the client has given\ninformed consent to it. Informed in this context means that in discussion (pre-test\ncounselling) the client has been made aware of all the ramifications of HIV testing, including\nthe risks and benefits, as well as of alternatives to such testing, in language he/she can\nunderstand. Consent means the giving of express agreement to. HIV testing in a situation\ndevoid of coercion, in which the client should feel equally free to grant or withhold consent.\n\nTesting without i",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Continues the glossary, defining testing with and without informed consent, voluntary testing and mandatory testing. It advises against the term 'routine testing' because it does not say whether informed consent is sought.",
